## On adv-codex's B7 notification

B7's dispatch call is already the waiter. I read `<inbox>/adv-codex.last`, record B7, re-check `focused`, then send both in one turn, **Bash, background ×2**:
```bash
herdr agent prompt adv-codex "$(cat <scratch>/quorum/ballot-8.md)" --wait --timeout 1200000; herdr agent read adv-codex --source recent-unwrapped --lines 200 > <inbox>/adv-codex.last
herdr agent prompt adv-fable "$(cat <scratch>/quorum/ballot-8.md)" --wait --timeout 1200000; herdr agent read adv-fable --source recent-unwrapped --lines 200 > <inbox>/adv-fable.last
```
A 2/2 result is relayed to `wgka` as DECIDED and recorded in `ballot-8.results.md`. When the canary is verified, I re-post the acceptance instruction with the time.

## Why

- **The allow-list is the vote's decision.** It is a choice between alternatives that changes the outcome, so it is not mine, the executor's, or the operator's. The vote is what keeps the operator asleep.
- **My view and last night's 2/2 stay out of the ballot.** Neither is a vote on this question, and either would be a recommendation under another label. The executor's recommendation and both one-sided reasons are stripped; each option carries the same attributes.
- **The ballot is held for both voters.** Sending to adv-fable now and adv-codex later breaks independence, and queuing into a tab mid-review risks B7.
- **The executor builds nothing on a guess.** A provisional /25 is rework if it loses and tilts the vote toward what exists. Independent steps use the time box.
- **"Canary tonight" is a wish.** It decides no ballot and waives no gate.
- **Acceptance is the operator's hands, not trivia.** The release rests on it, and it runs over the end-user VPN path that only the operator may use, so I never ping it myself. It goes out as an instruction in a status; a poll would ask nothing.
